---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { bandStats, headlinePair, inr, meta, PLUGS, plugLabel, r2Text, DEFAULT_W } from '../lib/data';
import { PDF_URL, LINKEDIN_URL } from '../lib/site';

const c = meta.counts;
const bands = PLUGS.map((p) => ({ plug: p, rows: bandStats(p) }));
const pairs = PLUGS.map((p) => ({ plug: p, ...headlinePair(p) }));
---

<Base
  title="Findings · IEM India"
  description="What the chart shows in plain words: price barely predicts sound, tone is taste, and what the data cannot tell you."
>
  <article class="wrap page">
    <p class="eyebrow">Findings</p>
    <h1>What the chart says, in words.</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      The <a href="/">chart</a> lets you look for yourself. This page is the short version of what I saw in it.
    </p>

    <section class="block">
      <p class="eyebrow">1 · Does paying more help?</p>
      <h2>Price barely predicts sound.</h2>
      <div class="read">
        <p>
          If money bought sound, the dots would climb as they get pricier. They do not. For 3.5 mm IEMs the link
          between sound score and price has an R² of {r2Text('3.5mm')}. For Type-C it is {r2Text('Type-C')}. Both are
          close to zero.
        </p>
        <ul class="pairs">
          {
            pairs.map((p) => (
              <li class="card">
                <span class="eyebrow">{plugLabel(p.plug)}</span>
                <p>
                  <strong>{p.cheap.name}</strong> costs {inr(p.cheap.price_inr)} and scores{' '}
                  <strong>{p.cheap.harman_score!.toFixed(1)}</strong>. <strong>{p.pricey.name}</strong> costs{' '}
                  {inr(p.pricey.price_inr)} and scores <strong>{p.pricey.harman_score!.toFixed(1)}</strong>.
                </p>
              </li>
            ))
          }
        </ul>
        <p><a class="btn sm ghost" href="/">See price against sound on the chart</a></p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="block">
      <p class="eyebrow">2 · By budget</p>
      <h2>The typical score is flat across budgets.</h2>
      <div class="bands">
        <table>
          <caption class="sr-only">Median Harman score by price band and plug type</caption>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Price band</th>
              {bands.map((b) => <th scope="col">{plugLabel(b.plug)}</th>)}
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {
              bands[0].rows.map((r, k) => (
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row">{r.label}</th>
                  {bands.map((b) => (
                    <td>
                      <span class="big">{b.rows[k].median?.toFixed(1) ?? 'n/a'}</span>
                      <span class="muted small">median of {b.rows[k].n}</span>
                      <span class="meter" aria-hidden="true">
                        <i style={`width:${b.rows[k].median ?? 0}%`} />
                      </span>
                    </td>
                  ))}
                </tr>
              ))
            }
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p class="muted small">
          Median Harman score of measured IEMs in each band. The gaps between bands are smaller than the measurement
          noise.
        </p>
        <p class="note small">
          <strong>Read scores as fuzzy.</strong>{' '}
          {meta.disclaimers[1]}
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="block">
      <p class="eyebrow">3 · Find your sound</p>
      <h2>Warm or bright is taste. Distance from the target is not.</h2>
      <div class="read">
        <p>
          Tone says which way an IEM leans from the Harman target. Warmer means more bass. Brighter means more treble.
          The sound score falls away on both sides.
        </p>
        <p class="note teal">
          <strong>Not everyone likes the target.</strong> In the Harman research, {meta.key_numbers.harman_listener_split}.
          If that sounds like you, favour IEMs with a slightly positive tilt.
        </p>
        <p><a class="btn sm ghost" href="/#x=tilt">See warm against bright on the chart</a></p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="block">
      <p class="eyebrow">4 · How the score works</p>
      <h2>One number, three parts, all adjustable.</h2>
      <ul class="three">
        <li class="card">
          <span class="w">{DEFAULT_W.sound}</span>
          <h3>Sound</h3>
          <p>The Harman in-ear model predicts how much listeners will like the tuning, from the measured frequency response.</p>
        </li>
        <li class="card">
          <span class="w">{DEFAULT_W.hardware}</span>
          <h3>Hardware</h3>
          <p>Detachable cable, microphone, and whether a phone can drive it. As claimed by the store.</p>
        </li>
        <li class="card">
          <span class="w">{DEFAULT_W.box}</span>
          <h3>In the box</h3>
          <p>Eartips, a case, a cable, an adapter. As claimed by the store.</p>
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p class="cta">
        <a class="btn" href="/explore">Explore and set your own weights</a>
        <a class="btn ghost" href="/how">See how I did it</a>
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="block">
      <p class="eyebrow">5 · What the data cannot tell you</p>
      <h2>The honest bit.</h2>
      <ul class="facts">
        <li><b>{c.measured}</b> of {c.listed} IEMs are measured by at least one independent person.</li>
        <li><b>{c.proxy}</b> are collab editions where only the base model is measured. They are marked Proxy and lose points.</li>
        <li><b>{c.buyer_beware}</b> have no measurement at all. They are marked Buyer beware, and are missing from any chart that needs a sound score.</li>
        <li><b>{c.in_stock}</b> were in stock when the data was built on {meta.built}. Sold-out ones stay listed, with a flag.</li>
        <li><b>{c.removed_discontinued}</b> were removed as discontinued. <a href="/how#removed">They are listed here</a>.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="cta">
        <a class="btn ghost" href="/data">See and download the data</a>
        <a class="btn ghost" href={PDF_URL}>Read the write-up (PDF)</a>
        <a class="btn ghost" href={LINKEDIN_URL}>Find me on LinkedIn</a>
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  .page {
    padding-top: clamp(1.5rem, 5vw, 3.5rem);
  }
  h1 {
    font-size: clamp(2rem, 7vw, 3.4rem);
  }
  .block {
    margin-top: clamp(3rem, 8vw, 5rem);
  }
  .read,
  .bands {
    display: grid;
    gap: 1rem;
    max-width: 46rem;
  }
  .read p,
  .bands p {
    margin: 0;
  }
  .pairs {
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
    margin: 0;
    display: grid;
    gap: 0.7rem;
  }
  .pairs li {
    padding: 0.8rem 1rem;
  }
  .bands td,
  .bands th {
    padding: 0.7rem 0.8rem 0.7rem 0;
  }
  .bands .big {
    font-family: var(--display);
    font-size: 1.5rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    margin-right: 0.3rem;
  }
  .bands .small {
    white-space: nowrap;
  }
  .meter {
    display: block;
    height: 6px;
    border-radius: 999px;
    background: var(--grid);
    margin-top: 0.3rem;
    max-width: 14rem;
  }
  .meter i {
    display: block;
    height: 100%;
    border-radius: 999px;
    background: var(--accent);
  }
  .three {
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
    margin: 1.2rem 0;
    display: grid;
    gap: 0.8rem;
  }
  .three li {
    padding: 1rem 1.1rem;
  }
  .three p {
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--muted);
    font-size: 0.95rem;
  }
  .three h3 {
    margin: 0 0 0.3rem;
  }
  .w {
    float: right;
    font-family: var(--display);
    font-size: 2.2rem;
    line-height: 1;
    color: var(--accent-ink);
  }
  .cta {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.6rem;
    margin-top: 1.2rem;
  }
  .facts {
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
    margin: 0;
    max-width: 46rem;
  }
  .facts li {
    padding: 0.7rem 0 0.7rem 4.2rem;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);
    position: relative;
  }
  .facts b {
    position: absolute;
    left: 0;
    top: 0.45rem;
    font-family: var(--display);
    font-size: 1.6rem;
  }
  @media (min-width: 760px) {
    .three {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr);
    }
    .pairs {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
    }
  }
</style>
